    {
      "id": "CARD-AM-08-concept",
      "conceptId": "C-AM-08",
      "kind": "concept",
      "title": {
        "en": "Carrier mismatch and debugging",
        "or": "Carrier mismatch ଓ Debugging"
      },
      "body": {
        "en": "Carrier mismatch is a receiver alignment problem, not automatically a failed transmitter. With equal carrier frequencies, a relative phase error phi scales the ideal recovered message by cos(phi) after gain correction. A 60 degree error leaves half amplitude; a 90 degree error suppresses the ideal message-band component. A finite filter can leave residual high-frequency ripple, so the observed trace need not be perfectly zero. A frequency mismatch adds a time-varying factor and can create a beating or distorted recovered waveform. Inspect the transmitter first, then compare the local oscillator frequency and phase with the carrier before changing unrelated blocks.",
        "or": "Carrier mismatch receiver ର alignment ସମସ୍ୟା ହୋଇପାରେ; ତେଣୁ transmitter ଖରାପ ବୋଲି ସିଧାସଳଖ ଧରନ୍ତୁ ନାହିଁ। Frequency ସମାନ ହେଲେ relative Phase error phi ଦ୍ୱାରା ideal recovered message ର Amplitude cos(phi) ଗୁଣ ହୁଏ। 60 degree error ରେ ଅଧା Amplitude ରହେ। 90 degree error ରେ ideal message-band ଅଂଶ କମି ଶୂନ୍ୟ ହୁଏ; finite Filter ପାଇଁ କିଛି high-frequency Ripple ରହିପାରେ। Frequency mismatch ରେ recovery ସମୟ ସହିତ ବଦଳିପାରେ। ପ୍ରଥମେ transmitter ଦେଖନ୍ତୁ, ତାପରେ Local oscillator ର Frequency ଓ Phase ମେଳାନ୍ତୁ।"
      },
